projects.description:
Turkmenistan made significant progress in control of communicable and non-communicable diseases. Within disease-specific programmes the major effort was on upgrading the treatment capacities, while laboratory capacities were given less priority (as elsewhere in the world). Laboratories are an essential and fundamental part of health systems and contribute directly to the improvement of health services. 
This project aims at strengthening the national health systems’ capacity for clinical laboratory diagnostics of diseases. The project will support health services to ensure availability, accessibility, reliability, and most-up-to-date laboratory investigations for accurate and timely diagnosis of the most common diseases, such as common infectious, non-infectious diseases, surgery, emergency cases, maternal and child health, and others. The project will focus on the laboratory capacities of the primary health care facilities, but secondary and tertiary care facilities will be also supported. The project will use the following approaches: 
•	Procurement of lab supplies (reagents, consumables, equipment) of guaranteed quality and safety, and at affordable for public health prices;
•	Introducing innovative lab technologies;
•	Development or update of the national lab SOPs and protocols;
•	Training of lab specialists on new lab protocols and new technologies; 
•	Exposing of Turkmen lab specialists to international best practices in lab diagnosis of various diseases by supporting their attendance of workshops, conferences, or organizing study visits.
•	Strengthening of the lab inventory, quantification and planning through digital solutions;
•	Ensuring regular calibration, maintenance and repairs of the lab equipment;
•	Monitoring of lab performance and introducing External Quality Assurance. 
The project will overall strengthen the health system’s capacities for public procurement for health products and services, for efficient quantification and forecasting; and will overall contribute to cost-efficiency gains of the state budget. 
Ultimately, the project will contribute to achieving the Sustainable Development Goal 3: Ensuring healthy lives and promoting well-being for all at all age, including SDG 3.8: Achieve universal health coverage, including financial risk protection, access to quality essential health-care services and access to safe, effective, quality and affordable essential medicines and vaccines for all. The impact of the project will extend beyond SDG3 and will contribute to SDG-5 Gender equality; SDG-10 Reduced inequality; SDG-17 Partnerships for the goal.

Specific activities include: 
- Support the implementation of the National ECD Strategy for 2020-2025 and its Action Plan: intersectoral coordination, monitoring the implementation of the ECD programme, and support the development of the new ECD strategy (MHMI, MOE, MLSP);
- Strengthening parental empowerment by developing evidence- and behavioral-change based solutions to improve pre-school preparation and participation of children in pre-primary education programmes (MoE);
-Promoting positive parenting by strengthening routine services to support responsive caregiving, nurturing care and the well-being of parents (MHMI).

The project "Strengthening Regional Collaboration and National Capacities for Management of Wheat Rust Diseases and Resistance Breeding in Central Asia and Caucasus (CACRust)" has been developed based on the request of the General Directorate of Agricultural Research and Policies (GDAR) of Türkiye. It will serve primarily seven countries in the Central Asia and Caucasus (CAC) region including, Republic of Azerbaijan, Republic of Kazakhstan, Kyrgyz Republic, Republic of Tajikistan, Republic of Türkiye, Turkmenistan and Uzbekistan. Where possible and needed, support will also be provided to additional countries in the Near East and North Africa such as the training and information sharing activities. Other opportunities will also be explored in this regard. For effective implementation of the project, collaborations with the national institutions and international organisations such as Regional Cereal Rust Research Center (RCRRC) based in Izmir, Türkiye, International Center for Agricultural Research in the Dry Areas (ICARDA) and International Wheat and Maize Improvement Center (CIMMYT) will be key. The project activities will complement other activities carried out in context of the Borlaug Global Rust Initiative (BGRI), of which FAO is also a member.

The project will support activities in the following domains: 
I.	Facilitation of regional collaboration and networking;
II.	Training of national technical officers;
III.	Facilitation of disease surveillance;
IV.	Race analysis and mapping through the global rust monitoring system;
V.	Characterization of the resistance properties of the popular varieties;
VI.	Supporting the breeding programmes for developing resistant varieties;
VII.	Integrated disease management;
VIII.	Development of contingency plans and strategies;
IX.	Strengthening physical capacities of institutions.

Turkmenistan Country Component of the Regional Project will be aimed at:
-	Empowering young people with low level of education by providing respective vocational training for employment along with providing mentoring support to increase their economic empowerment and strengthen community resilience;
-	Building national capacity in promoting employment opportunities as one of the directions in increasing resilience of communities to VE by enhancing technical capacity of Employment Departments of the MLSP to develop mechanisms of supporting vulnerable youth with the aim of addressing socio-economic vulnerability and building their resilience against violent extremism;
-	Enhancing capacities of MLSP, Youth Organization, Women Union, Law Enforcement at central and local levels and local administrations to enhance national ownership of PVE programs and strengthen local mechanisms with the aim of supporting communities to build resilience against violent extremism;  
-	Supporting youth in challenging contexts and their families with counselling and mentorship in response to early signs of radicalization.
